WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.530
Film Courage: Well I know you said that you
appreciate USC because the people in the documentary

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.530
[referring to an earlier video of people going
to a competitive graduate writing program]

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.910
said they also appreciated the school (they
still have nothing but respect for it) but

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.969
it was a very excruciating process and one
of the teachers even said “Well, if you

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:17.130
think this is difficult [the curriculum],
try being in the real world.”

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.580
Sort of this breeding ground, who is going
to make it, who is not.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.439
And some people didn’t make it.

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:25.650
I was wondering how that was for you because
it sounds like you have a very supportive

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:32.279
family and you came from a college where you
got your four year degree.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.570
What advice would you give to someone who
is coming here and they know that this is

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:41.410
just what they have to go through, it’s
not intentional, they are not trying to be

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.410
mean.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:51.359
Justin Warren, filmmaker: I’d say don’t
quit because it’s super easy to get discouraged

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:56.590
and to feel bitter and feel like “Oh these
people don’t get me.”

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:02.859
But the great part is that it does force you
to examine what you’re doing, to examine

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:09.130
your intention in your work, to sharpen it
so it’s the clearest, most precise version

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.020
of what you’re trying to do.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:19.290
And when you realize…when you take your
ego out of the process and you go into a note

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.880
session thinking “Okay, I’m just here
to get information.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.259
That information is so I can prove my work.”

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:33.780
It’s not going there to get validation that
your good at what you do, that’s not what

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.780
that process is for.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.070
Think of it as a process of whittling it down,
you’re whittling a stick into something

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:41.750
sharp.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:47.969
And then when you’re done with that whittling
process then you can sort of release to the

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:56.490
world and have it be something that your proud
of hopefully and that process is so critical

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:04.110
because when I think about how I used to work
as an artist, as a creator before my film

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.840
school experience, I would…if I was going
to make a short or something…because I made

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.530
a short before I went to school…and I made
one cut and that was it.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.410
I was like “Oh, cool.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.410
I’m done.”

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.520
Because it wa right there, I did it.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:21.480
But then you realize that in film school that’s
where it begins.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:28.970
That’s where the work starts is on your
first try where you thought you crushed it

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.160
and you realize “Oh, I actually didn’t
crush it at all?”

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.730
And that sense of kind of humility that come
with the craft is a really beautiful place

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.390
to be in (at least I feel that way).

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:49.100
And I now love that process.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.620
But it’s unfortunate because I saw some
of my friends didn’t make it in the program

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.970
because they were getting so much harsh feedback,
I think they started to take it personally

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:03.110
and they started to think”Well nobody gets
what I’m doing” and so they dropped out

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.540
of the program.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.690
And that’s very unfortunate.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:12.080
I mean I’m not saying that staying in…everybody
has their own path that’s what is best for

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:20.270
them, but when I wanted to quit, I was really,
really, grateful that I didn’t.because if

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:26.750
I would have quit right after that session
where I wept bitterly, I would have relieved

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:35.090
myself of some short term pain but I would
not be nearly the filmmaker that I am now.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:42.120
And the great part also about the notes process
is that it keeps you humble in the sense that

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:48.950
there is so much that you don’t know and
there is so much to be explored that the journey

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:54.920
to booming a good filmmaker or a good writer
is a lifelong process and wherever you are

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.850
in the process, that’s just where you are.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:06.171
And I’m grateful to the point where I’m
just now like “Okay, I’m going to try

00:04:06.171 --> 00:04:12.270
something new and I might fail but I’ve
gotten through failure before and I’m still

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.650
here so I know that I can get through it.

